# DigiSwasthya Product Definition Document (PDD)

# Part 3 — Business Entities

---

# Purpose

Every feature within DigiSwasthya revolves around business entities.

A business entity represents a real-world object or business record that exists within the pharmaceutical supply chain.

This section defines every entity that exists within the platform.

It intentionally does not describe user interfaces or implementation.

Instead, it defines what each entity represents, who owns it, how it relates to other entities, and its lifecycle within the product.

These definitions become the foundation for every workflow, business rule, audit, and future feature.

---

# What is a Business Entity?

A business entity is a record representing a real business object.

Examples include:

- A pharmacy.
- A stockist.
- An order.
- An invoice.
- A payment.
- A return.

Every feature within DigiSwasthya either creates, modifies, reads, or deletes one or more business entities.

No feature exists independently of these entities.

---

# Entity Principles

Every business entity follows these principles.

## Unique

Every entity should exist only once.

Duplicate records should never represent the same real-world business object.

---

## Traceable

Every significant change to an entity should be traceable.

The platform should always know:

- what changed
- when it changed
- who changed it

---

## Owned

Every entity has one primary owner.

Other users may interact with it depending on permissions, but ownership remains clearly defined.

---

## Related

Entities are connected.

For example:

An invoice belongs to an order.

A payment belongs to one or more invoices.

A return belongs to delivered products.

A pharmacy belongs to a business account.

---

# Core Business Entities

The current DigiSwasthya product consists of the following core entities.

---

# Business

Represents a legally registered organisation using DigiSwasthya.

A business may operate as:

- Pharmacy
- Stockist

A business owns nearly every other entity created within the platform.

---

# User

Represents an individual person who accesses the platform.

A user always belongs to exactly one business.

A user never exists independently.

Users perform actions on behalf of their business.

---

# Business Verification

Represents the verification status of a business.

Verification determines whether a business may participate in trading activities.

Every business has exactly one current verification status.

---

# Connection

Represents an approved trading relationship between one pharmacy and one stockist.

Connections determine who may trade with whom.

Orders cannot exist without an active connection.

---

# Product

Represents a medicine or healthcare product available for trade.

Products are offered by stockists.

Multiple stockists may offer the same medicine independently.

Product information may vary between suppliers.

---

# Product Catalogue

Represents the complete list of products offered by a stockist.

Every stockist owns one catalogue.

Pharmacies browse catalogues when placing orders.

---

# Inventory

Represents physical stock currently available.

Inventory belongs to a business.

Inventory increases and decreases through business activities.

Inventory should always reflect actual physical stock.

---

# Batch

Represents a manufacturing batch of a product.

Each batch contains:

- quantity
- batch number
- manufacturing details
- expiry information

Inventory is maintained at the batch level.

---

# Order

Represents a purchase request sent by a pharmacy to a stockist.

An order contains one or more products.

An order progresses through multiple business states before completion.

Orders represent intent to purchase.

They do not represent financial settlement.

---

# Invoice

Represents the financial document generated for an order.

Invoices define what must be paid.

Invoices exist independently from payments.

---

# Payment

Represents money paid by a pharmacy to a stockist.

Payments reduce outstanding invoice balances.

A payment may settle one or multiple invoices depending on business rules.

---

# Return

Represents products requested to be returned after delivery.

Returns do not immediately affect payments.

Returns require approval before financial adjustment.

---

# Credit Note

Represents financial credit issued after an approved return or other adjustment.

Credit notes reduce future invoice liabilities.

They do not directly refund money unless future product rules explicitly support refunds.

---

# Delivery

Represents the transportation of products from stockist to pharmacy.

A delivery is responsible for moving products associated with one or more invoices or orders.

Delivery completion does not necessarily indicate payment completion.

---

# Notification

Represents information delivered to users regarding business events.

Notifications never modify business data.

They only communicate events.

---

# Message

Represents communication between platform users.

Messages assist business communication.

Messages do not replace official business records such as orders, invoices, or payments.

---

# Support Ticket

Represents a request for assistance submitted by a business.

Support tickets remain independent from business transactions.

---

# Analytics

Represents calculated business insights.

Analytics are derived from business data.

Analytics never become the source of truth.

The underlying business records always remain authoritative.

---

# Relationships Between Entities

```
Business
│
├── Users
├── Verification
├── Inventory
├── Product Catalogue
├── Staff
├── Addresses
└── Settings

Connection
│
├── Pharmacy
└── Stockist

Order
│
├── Pharmacy
├── Stockist
├── Products
└── Delivery

Invoice
│
└── Order

Payment
│
└── Invoice(s)

Return
│
└── Delivered Order Items

Credit Note
│
└── Approved Return

Notification
│
└── Business Event

Support Ticket
│
└── Business
```

---

# Source of Truth

Every entity has exactly one authoritative record.

Derived information should never replace the original entity.

Examples:

Inventory dashboards are derived from inventory records.

Sales analytics are derived from completed transactions.

Outstanding balances are derived from invoices and payments.

Notifications are derived from business events.

Dashboards, charts, summaries, and reports should always reference the underlying entities rather than maintain separate copies of the same information.

---

# Future Expansion

Future versions of DigiSwasthya may introduce additional entities.

Examples include:

- Purchase Orders
- Manufacturers
- Logistics Providers
- Warehouses
- Claims
- Insurance
- Compliance Records

These future entities should integrate into the existing domain model without changing the definitions of the core entities described in this document.